{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Sister of Nevayot.</b> From the fact that it says 'daughter of Yishmael,' do I not already know that she is the sister of Nevayot? Rather, it teaches that Yishmael died after he betrothed her to Esav, before her marriage, and Nevayot her brother married her off. And we learn that Yaakov was sixty-three years old at that time — for Yishmael was seventy-four years old when Yaakov was born: he was fourteen years older than Yitzchak, and Yitzchak was sixty at their birth, that makes seventy-four. And his years were one hundred thirty-seven, as it says, ״וְאֵלֶּה שְׁנֵי חַיֵּי יִשְׁמָעֵאל״ and so forth. So Yaakov was sixty-three when Yishmael died. And from here we learn that he stayed hidden in the house of Ever fourteen years, and afterward went to Haran — for he tarried in the house of Lavan no more than fourteen years before the birth of Yosef, as it says, ״עֲבַדְתִּיךָ אַרְבַּע עֶשְׂרֵה שָׁנָה בִּשְׁתֵּי בְנֹתֶיךָ וְשֵׁשׁ שָׁנִים בְּצֹאנֶךָ,״ and the wage for the flock was from when Yosef was born, as it says, ״וַיְהִי כַּאֲשֶׁר יָלְדָה רָחֵל אֶת יוֹסֵף״ and so forth. And Yosef was thirty when he became king, and from there until Yaakov went down to Egypt was nine years — seven of plenty and two of famine. And Yaakov said to Pharaoh, ״The days of my sojourning are thirty and a hundred years.״ Go and calculate: fourteen before Yosef's birth, thirty of Yosef, and nine from when he became king until Yaakov came — that makes fifty-three. And when he parted from his father he was sixty-three — that makes one hundred sixteen. But he says one hundred thirty-three years — that is short fourteen years. You have thus learned that after he received the blessings he stayed hidden in the house of Ever fourteen years, but was not punished, in the merit of Torah. For Yosef parted from his father only twenty-two years — that is, from seventeen to thirty-nine — corresponding to the twenty-two years that Yaakov parted from his father and did not honor him: twenty years in the house of Lavan and two years that he tarried on the way, as it says, ״וַיִּבֶן לוֹ בַּיִת וּלְמִקְנֵהוּ עָשָׂה סֻכֹּת,״ and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded from this verse that he tarried eighteen months on the way — 'house' refers to the rainy season and 'succot' to the sunny season — and for the reckoning of the verses we calculated above, from when he parted from his father until he went down to Egypt he was one hundred thirty years old, where we find another fourteen years. Rather, certainly he stayed hidden in the house of Ever on his way to the house of Lavan, to learn Torah from him, and for the merit of Torah he was not punished for them, and Yosef parted from him only twenty-two years — measure for measure — thus far in old Rashi." "<b>Over his wives.</b> He added wickedness to his wickedness — for he did not divorce the first ones."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "This is a single extended chronological comment that Rashi attributes at its close to רש״י ישן ('old Rashi'), indicating he is quoting an earlier source; the entire passage including that closing attribution is rendered as found."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:01:04.939717842Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}